// Four sonorities, and what a reading can and cannot settle about them.
//
// The point of this fixture is that `musa analyze --kind chords` argues rather
// than picking, and that where it *can* settle something it says so.
//
// Bar 1 — C, E, G, A. Two chords fit it exactly: a C major triad with an added
// sixth, and an A minor seventh in first inversion (OMT `018-seventh-chords.md`,
// `019-inversion.md`). Which one it is depends on what the passage is doing,
// which a single slice does not know. Both are candidates, in one report.
//
// Bar 2 — C and G alone. Every chord those two notes are two-thirds of is a
// candidate at an `incomplete` fit: C major, C minor, C sus2, C sus4, and
// G sus4 over C. A voicing may leave a chord member out (OMT
// `076-jazz-voicings.md` §Omitting Notes), and a third left out takes the
// quality with it.
//
// Bar 3 — a triad with a fourth note sounding through it. The fit is `with
// extra tones`. Nothing here decides that the extra note is embellishing
// (OMT `039-embellishing-tones.md`): that is a reading of a *line*, and this
// analysis reads a slice.
//
// Bar 4 — a fully diminished seventh, spelled B, D, F, A-flat. A listener
// hears four possible roots here; this reading reports one, because chord
// membership is by *spelling* and the other three rootings are spelled with
// notes this bar does not contain. That is a stated limit of the analysis
// (`docs/language/07-analysis.md` §7), not an oversight: re-spelling the score
// to find more readings would be constructing music, which an analysis may
// never do.
piece "Equivocal Sonority" {
    composer "musa";

    tempo 1/4 = 60;
    meter 4/4;
    key c major;

    score {
        part piano {
            clef treble;

            voice upper {
                | [c4 e4 g4 a4]/1
                | [c4 g4]/1
                | [c4 e4 g4 d5]/1
                | [b3 d4 f4 ab4]/1
            }
        }
    }
}
